message.content:
{
  "summary": "Action potentials typically initiate in the axon initial segment due to high voltage-gated channel density, where depolarization triggers sodium influx followed by potassium efflux and inactivation to terminate the spike. The absolute refractory period prevents immediate re-firing, while the relative refractory period requires stronger inputs. These channel-dependent mechanisms limit firing patterns and constrain a neuron's capacity to process closely spaced inputs.",
  "topics": [
    "Axon initial segment as action potential initiation site",
    "Voltage-gated sodium and potassium channel dynamics",
    "Absolute and relative refractory periods",
    "Constraints on neuronal firing patterns and input processing"
  ]
}

